Interesting view. (Are you counting object types or
number of individual objects? - In both cases, your
statement seems to not fit my own experiences very
well, though...)

We have 22 types of scrolls, about 15 useful scroll
types, and about 7 very useful scroll types.

We have 28 types of potions, about 15 useful potion
types, and about 7 very useful potion types.

We have 28 types of rings, about 20 useful ring types,
and about 7 very useful ring types.

We have 24 types of wands, about 17 useful wand types,
and about 7 very useful wand types.

And we have a lot more useful and very useful items
from the book object class, from the tools, and more.

(My impression is that all summed up useful and very
useful objects in my games do exceed the number from
monster equipment. But the individual object classes
show some differences...)

From the scroll types we find just two (actually one
more, but scroll of earth is rare) in monster equipment;
create monster may be useful early on when you need
fresh corpses to get your artifact weapon, but sadly
you collect the tons of this scroll later, not when
you need them; and scrolls of teleportation, which
are indeed one from the set of most useful scrolls.

In my games I typically use much more types of useful
scrolls from random sources than from monster inventory.
And the sum of all individually counted scrolls of all
useful scrolls is also larger than the summed amount of
the single useful scroll type from the monster's equipment.

A different story are the potions; from the class of 7
most useful potion types you may get all the 5 potion
types necessary for the full healing recipe from monster
inventory as well, and you may also get them in large
quantities - i.e. *if* you manage to kill the monster
before it can quaff the potion.

If I consider all the useful and most useful potions
in my games, the potions from monster equipment do
significantly contribute.

Rings is again somewhat different; you typically need
just a single instance, and every new ring of the same
type is often just redundant. Similar with amulets.
Those redundant pieces are (similar to non-useful items
from other object classes) often just used as polymorph
fodder, thus have some limited usefulness.

Wands from monster inventory are similar to potions;
they are as well an interesting and significant source.
You may get many of the best wands; fire, lightning,
digging, sleep, or teleportation (to name the most
interesting ones). The problem here is similar to the
potions from monster inventory; monster use those wands
to attack you or use those items to escape.

> 
> Note that majority of useful items is not the same
> thing as the _most useful_ items.  The most useful
> item in the game is the wand of wishing,

(This is relative to the horizon you consider; if some
immediate danger is threatening your life you may be
better served with a wand of digging, or teleportation,
or fire, etc. The wand of wishing is certainly the
universally most useful if you have at least the time
to make use of the wished for object.)

> which in
> most games is only found where guaranteed, at
> the castle.  The second-most-useful item in the
> game is probably a magic marker, which is usually
> from either wishing or bones or occasionally a
> result of random object generation.

(I think _for discussion purposes_ both of the sources
you mention (wishing and bones) are not helpful. Since
you can wish for quite everything this is a triviality.
And since bones are just quasi a projection of some
previously played game, you shift answering the object
generation argument from from one game to the next
without that it would explain anything. So I suggest
to focus on the actual _primary_ sources of items;
mainly, random objects from dungeon generation, random
objects from death-drops, and monster equipment.)

> 
